<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<mods>
  <!--
        Gigantic is tinkerable here, which vanilla does not allow - it ships TinkerAllowed="false"
        so the mod is found and lived with rather than built. Keeping the capability means paying
        for it, and TinkerTier is where that happens.

        Tier 7 is the top of what vanilla lets anyone build, alongside ModNanon and ModSuspensor.
        Nothing lower is defensible: ModGigantic.ApplyModification calls AdjustDamage(3) on a melee
        weapon and damage is rolled ONCE PER PENETRATION, so it is +3 per penetration rather than
        +3 flat - and it doubles energy cell capacity, grenade radius and tonic dosage besides.
        Every tinkerable vanilla mod at tier 6 or 7 carries a value multiplier of 1.3 to 1.5 and
        grants far less. Without this attribute ModEntry defaults TinkerTier to 1. See #317.

        What the player pays is real and easy to miss, which is why the capability is worth
        keeping at all: GetSlotsRequiredEvent does E.Increases++, so a gigantic item takes an extra
        equipment slot unless the wielder is a gigantic creature - a gigantic one-handed sword
        needs both hands. And GetAddedWeight is the blueprint's weight x4, floored at 4.

        Load="Merge" is inert here: ModificationFactory.LoadModNode merges by Part and overwrites
        only stated attributes, so this would behave identically without it. Kept because
        merge-discipline asks for it on every vanilla record, and being explicit costs nothing.
    -->
  <mod Part="ModGigantic" Load="Merge" TinkerAllowed="true" TinkerTier="7" />

  <!--
        Six vanilla item mods are complete, gated, presented - and reachable by nothing. Each is the
        second rung of a ladder whose first rung ships and is tinkerable today. These are the four
        that are genuinely rungs: a further bonus on the same axis as their base. See #723.

        ModOverbuilt and ModSmart are deliberately left out. Overbuilt is not an upgrade but a
        TRADE (+2 AV for -2 DV, +10 speed penalty and double weight), and Smart converts a scoped
        gun into a powered artifact needing an energy cell, a boot sequence and a HUD on the wielder
        before its bonus applies at all. The tinker tree has no way to say "this one costs you
        something", so presenting either as a rung would be a lie told by the UI.

        Tables is MANDATORY and is not only about loot. TinkerData.CanMod iterates
        ModEntry.TableList and returns true only on a match - an absent Tables attribute yields an
        empty array, so the recipe would be learnable, visible in the tinker screen, and applicable
        to nothing, forever. #723's own plan had it as "tinker-only, a decision rather than an
        omission"; it is a dead entry.

        MinTier="99" is what keeps them out of loot instead. ModEntry.MinTier is read in exactly two
        places: ModificationFactory line 365, which gates whether a mod may be rolled onto a
        generated item, and RandomAltarBaetyl, which gates the Sparking Baetyl on MinTier <= 8. So
        one attribute closes both. (Generation could not produce them anyway - eligibility is
        computed once against the bare item and ModificationApplicable requires the base mod - but
        being explicit costs nothing and the baetyl exclusion is real.)

        TinkerTier does four jobs at once, which is why each is set deliberately rather than left to
        ModEntry's default of 1: it places the recipe in the tinker tree, sets data-disk rarity
        through DataDisk.GetDataScore, prices the water ritual at 50 x Tier / 3, and picks the
        required skill through DataDisk.GetRequiredSkill - Tinker I at 3 or below, Tinker II at 6
        or below, Tinker III above. Every value here lands in Tinker II, one rank above the
        Tinker I that Sharp, Masterwork and Serrated sit at. That rank is the real gate: the
        schematic says what to do and the rank is whether you are good enough to do it.

        Value is the item's price multiplier and is set one step above each base's, since a keen
        blade is a sharp blade plus more.

        No Rarity attribute: getRarityCode throws on anything it does not know and the default is
        fine, but more to the point rarity only matters to generation, which MinTier already closes.
    -->
  <mod
    Part="ModKeen"
    Tables="BladeMods,ThrownWeaponMods"
    MinTier="99"
    TinkerTier="4"
    TinkerDisplayName="keen"
    TinkerCategory="melee weapons"
    Value="1.2"
    Description="Keen: +1 to penetration rolls, in addition to sharp."
  />

  <mod
    Part="ModLegendary"
    Tables="WeaponMods,MissileWeaponMods,ThrownWeaponMods"
    MinTier="99"
    TinkerTier="5"
    TinkerDisplayName="legendary"
    TinkerCategory="melee weapons"
    Value="1.4"
    Description="Legendary: This weapon is 20% more likely to score critical hits, in addition to masterwork."
  />

  <mod
    Part="ModMicroserrated"
    Tables="LongBladeMods,AxeMods"
    MinTier="99"
    TinkerTier="6"
    TinkerDisplayName="microserrated"
    TinkerCategory="melee weapons"
    Value="1.5"
    Description="Microserrated: This weapon has a 7% chance to dismember opponents, in addition to serrated."
  />

  <mod
    Part="ModMassivelyOverloaded"
    Tables="CommonMods"
    MinTier="99"
    TinkerTier="6"
    CanAutoTinker="false"
    TinkerDisplayName="massively overloaded"
    TinkerCategory="utility"
    Value="1.5"
    Description="Massively overloaded: This item's power load is greatly increased, in addition to overloaded."
  />
</mods>
